Situation:
Regional Command is sustaining dispersed maneuver and fires units across multiple forward operating bases. Supply originates from several rear-area depots (e.g., Supply Depot Bravo, Depot Falcon, Logistics Support Area Rhino) with stocks varying by day and commodity. Supported units include infantry battalions, armored squadrons, and artillery batteries at locations such as FOB Atlas, COP Viper, and Fire Base Echo. Ground movement relies on designated main and alternate supply routes that traverse chokepoints, valleys, and urban corridors with varying threat levels. Convoys require engineer route clearance, electronic countermeasures, and armed escort. Weather and terrain constrain speeds and favor consolidated movements. Enemy activity is intermittent but capable of disrupting unescorted or lightly protected convoys.

Mission:
Deliver required classes of supply to all supported units over the planning horizon while minimizing total operational cost, to include transport costs, shortage penalties, vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs. Maintain combat readiness by prioritizing critical commodities and routes while respecting depot availability and fleet limits.

Execution/Concept:
- Supply Allocation: Each depot releases commodity flows to supported units by day, not to exceed available stocks at that depot and time. Unit demands vary by day; shortfalls are permitted only as a last resort and will accrue explicit penalty costs.
- Vehicle Utilization: Loads are consolidated into ground vehicles. Each vehicle has a fixed carrying capacity; different supply types consume capacity according to their specific volume factors. Vehicle deployment incurs a per-vehicle cost.
- Route Activation and Risk: Movement from a given depot to a given unit on a given day is conducted via an assigned route. Activating a route (sending any vehicles) incurs a fixed security risk cost for that route-day regardless of the number of vehicles employed. This risk cost represents the overhead of route clearance teams, escort elements, ISR coverage, and command-and-control coordination. Vehicles cannot be assigned to a route unless it is activated.
- Fleet Management: The total number of vehicles employed across all depot–unit movements on any day cannot exceed the available fleet. Consolidation of flows onto fewer activated routes can reduce fixed risk costs, but must be balanced against transport distances, per-unit transport costs, and demand urgency.
- Prioritization and Risk Mitigation: Critical ammunition and fuel deliveries to engaged units take precedence. Where risk or capacity constraints limit throughput, non-critical demands may be deferred and penalized accordingly. Planners will prefer secure corridors where feasible; higher-risk corridors are activated only when cost-effective relative to penalties and transport burden.
- Objective Function: The distribution plan selects daily flows, vehicle assignments, and route activations that minimize the sum of variable transport costs, shortage penalty costs, per-vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs while satisfying all capacity and fleet constraints.

